+HELP_TEXT = """Detectors and PIE-window definition Guide:
+
+You can select an existing detector Pulsed-Interleaved Excitation (PIE) window
+definition using the Setup dropdown, or define your own PIE and detector settings
+by editing the tables below.
+
+• TTTR Reading Routine:
+  Specify the file type and time resolution parameters used when reading TTTR files.
+  Clicking "Read" inspects a data file and automatically extracts resolutions and
+  populates the micro-time decay preview.
+
+• Micro-time Units:
+  PIE windows (Start / End) and detector Micro Time Ranges are given in RAW micro-time
+  channels of the TTTR file (typically 0 … a few thousand), i.e. the SAME units as the
+  loaded data. They are NOT divided by the micro-time binning.
+
+• PIE Windows:
+  Defines time windows separating prompt excitation from delayed (interleaved) excitation
+  pulses (e.g. prompt vs delayed laser pulses).
+
+• Detectors:
+  Maps physical detector channels (SPADs/APDs) to logical names (green, red, yellow, etc.),
+  assigns micro-time filter intervals, and applies polarization / detection efficiency
+  corrections (G-factor, l1, l2).
+
+• Polarization Resolved:
+  When enabled, detectors split parallel and perpendicular channels (VV / VH) for anisotropy.
+  When disabled, channels are combined without polarization splitting.
+
+• TAC Linearization (LUT):
+  Applies non-linearity look-up tables to calibrate TAC/TDC channels (e.g. for SPC-130).
+
+• Saving:
+  Click "Save" to persist changes to the detector setups repository.
+  Click "Proceed to 1. Data Selection ➔" (or Finish) to commit settings to the workflow.
+"""
 
